[#:seon.task{:ns seon.heap
             :name "a-heap-reading-counts-non-heap-pools-and-uncollected-eden"
             :plan "The heap watch's readings are not heap. `seon.heap/reading` sums the after-collection usage of every pool `com.sun.management.GcInfo/getMemoryUsageAfterGc` reports: the non-heap pools (Metaspace, CodeHeap, Compressed Class Space, about 290 MB on default) and, for a `G1 Concurrent GC` notification (counted as major), the eden that cycle never collects (117 MB in one reading). Measured on default 577091e04 (live-run-10, 2026-10-18): the 8 growth crossings recorded at 04:54 to 05:12 UTC (process 12196) read 1,139 to 1,405 MB against a 2,577 MB warning bound, none over it, none with an evaluation in flight, while `seon.cluster.status` reported about 600 MB live; the last concurrent cycle's after-usage was old gen 597 MB, eden 117 MB and about 290 MB non-heap. So the warning and critical bounds compare an inflated number against the heap's cap, and the growth rule (`seon.heap/step`) fires on metaspace and eden variation during warm-up, which marks the cluster `:faulted` with no pressure. Fix at the producer: a reading sums only the pools the reporting collector manages (`java.lang.management.GarbageCollectorMXBean/getMemoryPoolNames` of the bean named by `getGcName`: the concurrent cycle manages the old gen alone; the young and full collectors manage eden, survivor and old). Proof: `seon.heap-test/a-reading-counts-only-the-heap-its-collector-manages`. Host-bound boot code: a platform slice (`--test seon.heap-test`), proven by the next restart's status."
             :proofs #{seon.heap-test/a-reading-counts-only-the-heap-its-collector-manages}}]
